<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Copais</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kopais</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Copais was a large shallow lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, fed by the Cephissus river. In the Mycenaean period the people of Orchomenos drained much of it with dykes, canals and channels into natural sinkholes, one of the great engineering works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. The works later failed and the lake returned, until it was finally drained in the late 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-copais.jpg" alt="Lake Copais" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: georg-hessen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Llac_copais_abans_segle_XIX.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Copais, also spelled Kopais or Kopaida, was a lake in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. The towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haliartus">Haliartus</a>, Orchomenus and Chaeronea stood on its shores, and the rivers that fed it included the Cephissus, the Termessus and the Triton. The lake was surrounded by fertile land, but it increasingly encroached on that land because of inadequate drainage. Before the lake was drained, it emptied into the sea by numerous subterranean channels, some of them artificial, as the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> recorded in the 1st century. A one-time island in the lake was modified in ancient times into a megalithic citadel, now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gla">Gla</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Mycenaeans drained the lake partially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> by diverting some of its inflow towards natural sinkholes at its eastern end. They did so in at least two phases. First they built low earth dams about 22 km long, moving up to 400,000,000 cubic metres of stone and roughly 200,000,000 cubic metres of earth. After the dams failed, they built a canal about 25 km long carrying water towards sinkholes northeast of the lake, which divided the basin in two and led to partial reclamation of its southern part. The system collapsed by the Late Helladic IIIB or IIIC at the latest.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> mentions work on these channels by an engineer named Crates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, and two Roman attempts are documented, one attributed to a local man, Epameinondas Epameinondou, and one to the Emperor Hadrian.</p><p>Plans to drain the lake in modern times began in 1834-1838. In 1865 a French company signed a contract with the Greek government, but it went bankrupt in 1873 after draining the lake partially. A new contract with another French company in 1880 led to massive works between 1882 and 1886, which failed when burning organic material on the lake bottom lowered the lake bed about 4 metres below the drainage canal, and by 1887 the lake had filled again. A British company, Lake Copais Co Ltd, was then contracted and recovered 241 square kilometres of land. The Greek government expropriated the area in 1953 and redistributed it to landless farmers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The former lake is now flat dry land, still known as Kopaida. The Kopais Lake Agency was created in 1957 to supervise the drainage and the building of a new road. In ancient legend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> created the lake by flooding the area with the river Cephissus in order to unhorse the Minyans of Orchomenus, and Pausanias and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a> record that in antiquity it was known for its fish, especially its eels.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Copais” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Boeotia was one of the best farming regions of Greece, with wheat and barley lands around <b>Lake Copais</b> and the Theban and Tanagran plains, good horse pasture and the eels of Lake Copais, a delicacy prized at Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Engineers drained much of <b>Lake Copais</b> in Boeotia with dikes and canals to create farmland, and built roads with bridges of Cyclopean masonry, such as the bridge at Arkadiko on the road between Tiryns and Epidaurus, which still stands.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Orchomenos, on the north-west edge of <b>Lake Copais</b> in Boeotia, was an important settlement from the Early Bronze Age and gave its name to the grey 'Minyan ware' pottery.</blockquote><a href="/state/mycenaean-kingdom-of-orchomenos">Mycenaean Kingdom of Orchomenos</a></li><li><blockquote>After the collapse of the Mycenaean Kingdom of Orchomenos c. 1200 BCE, the town survived beside the refilled <b>Lake Copais</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/orchomenus-boeotia">Orchomenus (Boeotia)</a></li></ul></section>
